[{:seon.task/name "every-error-is-built-by-its-constructor"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by data's keys-schemas sweep, 2026-11-12: six error types are built as literal maps with :seon.error/type, never through `seon.contract/error`, so nothing checks them against their schema entry (the class seon.contract-test/every-error-type-a-raise-site-names-is-registered closed for the constructor's callers): :seon.lib/sysmem-refused (`seon.lib/sysmem`), :seon.render.value/insufficient-floor-budget-error and :seon.render.value/charge-exceeded (seon.print.select), :seon.task/refused (`seon.task/invalid`), :seon.fork-server/not-single-threaded. The fix: each site calls seon.contract/error, and a law refuses a map literal holding :seon.error/type outside seon.contract (read from the forms' :form/keywords and their text, proportional to the changed forms). Each type has its entry (bf87652eac)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.contract-test/no-error-map-is-built-outside-its-constructor}}
 {:seon.task/name "atoms-data"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-12: an atom is a proc not yet made (AGENTS.md section 3 step 4; docs/flow.md#the-step-fn-contract-every-proc-follows). Done: `seon.contract/within?` and `seon.contract/plain?` (with `seon.contract/kept`, its budget now an argument) thread their budget as a value; `seon.vector/coarse-rows` is a loop carrying its place and results; `seon.doc/baseline-reads` reads each old file once into a map before its walk. Left, each with its reason: the compile memo of `seon.contract/registry-of` and `seon.contract/registry-with` stays, since malli resolves a form's names through the registry's -schema callback during its own walk, so the memo must be filled there (the mapping cannot express it; said in their docstrings when this lands). `seon.contract/private-checks` becomes an option of the fork (a test child's), read at the child's load, once runtime's fork-one carries the options; `seon.contract/check-private!` goes with it. The text index (`seon.doc/acquire!`, `seon.doc/release!`, `seon.sqlite/fts-open`'s state, `seon.sqlite/replace-rows!`'s rollback flag) becomes one owner proc, and the embedding helper's handle in seon.vector the fx worker's state: runtime's proc work decides both."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.contract-test/data-namespaces-hold-no-atom-but-a-procs}}
 {:seon.task/name "contracts-armed-at-definition"
  :seon.task/plan "Every definition happens once (docs/resolution.md). Built: seon.contract's defn, defn- and defchecked define a contracted var checked from its definition (`contracted-def`: the var holds `checked`, direct-linked and inlined callers call the checked body), and the contract-gap rule is one, `seon.merge/uncontracted`, which the writer's merge gate and root's judge both ask. Still armed after the definition, by alter-var-root: an agent's REPL defn (`seon.contract/arm!`, from seon.repl's definition handling), the program loaded from the db (seon.program calls `arm!` and `rearm!`), and the foundation namespaces at boot (`seon.contract/instrument!` over `boot-nss`, in seon.system); the ::wrapper metadata (`instrument-keys`) keeps the raw root such a wrapper wraps. Correct: a definition carrying :malli/schema is defined already checked, from its definition event, in those three places; arm!, rearm!, instrument! and the ::wrapper metadata go. The generated vars of defrecord/defprotocol are recognised from the definition events."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.contract-test/a-contracted-definition-is-checked-from-its-definition
                      seon.contract-test/one-contract-gap-rule-agrees-everywhere}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-contract-is-checked-once-and-costs-nothing"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit's finding that exact :calls bounds make a new contract a cost regression (8c30ac5600 and 9c8b3d016c deleted contracts to save calls): \"Oh yeah contracts don't count against anything as long as you do it through the optimal interface and we aren't checking it multiple times for no reason. So understand what instrumentation does ensure we are instrumenting them and then if that is covering it then we don't need to check it multiple times if it's the same data.\" (docs/decisions.md). Four steps, in order. (1) What instrumentation does: how seon.contract/defn and seon.contract/wrap check args, returns and arity, where it is on (:seon.config.contract/instrument, the seven boot namespaces armed by seon.contract/instrument!, agents by fork), and what it misses: fns defined outside the defn macro, private fns, multimethods, protocol methods, anything excluded; written in the contract section of the wiki. (2) One interface: every contract is checked through that instrumentation; hand m/validate and m/explain calls and other ad hoc checks in src go through it or are deleted where the instrumented contract already covers them; each gap found in (1) is filled, so every contracted fn is instrumented. (3) No double checks: the same data validated more than once for no reason (a return checked and then the same value as the next fn's arg under the same schema; a hand check beside an instrumented one; a value re-checked each pass of a loop) is a class: find the cases, then design the mechanism that removes it (not case patches), and bring the design to the owner, through the context session, before building it. (4) Only then seon.cost stops counting the contract's own calls in :calls (seon.contract/wrap's counted wrapper); every pinned number moves once, approved under this ruling (the 616 and 8 included), and the merge report lists each old and new number. After it, a new contract on the one interface costs nothing on any proof, and a redundant re-check is still waste. Docs: docs/cost.md and the contract section. (5) What one check costs (measured 2026-11-10, Context, for the owner's ruling \"Private helpers: tests only\" in docs/decisions.md): a contracted call costs about 70 engine ticks over its raw call (a small value's print 25.8k ticks with contracts on, 12.9k off); a compiled validator per argument should be a few ticks, so a check of a one-argument function with a simple schema should cost about 10. Find where the 70 go by profiling seon.contract/wrap's checked function on one call (the arity dispatch, the args vector, each argument's validator, the return's, seon.profile's counting, apply) and cut it at its cause, so that checking public functions on every call costs what a check is worth. The cost bounds that count checks rather than work (their calls fall tenfold with private checks off while their ticks stay the same: seon.ctx-test/a-callers-window-costs-its-forms 33 calls in test, 3 in production, 2.67M ticks either way; a-window-moves-by-its-change 21/2; the-patch-costs-the-change-not-the-session 21/2; a-lookups-patch-costs-its-change 9/1) are restated in ticks only with the owner's yes (step 4)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.contract-test/every-contracted-fn-is-instrumented seon.cost-test/a-contracts-own-calls-are-not-counted seon.contract-test/a-check-costs-its-validators}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-boundary-takes-keyed-schemas"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by docs/merging.md#a-landing-message-carries-its-audit its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: contracts are armed everywhere, but what they say is loose: of about 1,780 :malli/schema in src about 125 answer :any and 151 answer :map, about 460 take a bare :map; :seon.error/base and entity-fact maps are open, so any extra key with any value passes. The change: a var property {:seon/boundary true}, starting with the edge functions of docs/failure-points.md, the store writers and the wire (server/answer, web/ui-call, model/sse-reply, model/request-body, repl/record!, my.root/load-example!); a boundary takes and answers named keyed schemas, no bare :map, :any or host object; bin/check contracts reports the count of loose returns and arguments with a ratchet that may only fall; maps the system writes are closed ({:closed true} on entity facts and error variants; [:map-of :keyword :seon.db/plain] where extension is wanted; agents' own schemas stay open); each tagged kind has one checked constructor (not built: seon.render/as [k v], planned in seon.render/a-render-tag-is-a-checked-schema; today :seon.render/as is only the output-kind option key). The rule is written in docs/checks.md#contracts-armed-at-definition and docs/decisions.md, with the owner's words. The cost bounds are settled first by seon.contract/a-contract-is-checked-once-and-costs-nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.render/a-render-tag-is-a-checked-schema" "seon.contract/a-contract-is-checked-once-and-costs-nothing"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.contract-test/a-loose-boundary-is-counted}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-bare-schema-read-on-a-branch-reads-the-branch", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/proofs #{seon.contract-test/a-bare-schema-read-on-a-branch-reads-the-branch seon.agent-test/an-agents-start-costs-the-same-however-long-its-root-has-run}, :seon.task/estimate #:seon.contract{options {:n 1, :order :constant, :ticks 10000, :bytes 100000}}, :seon.task/plan "Owner: data. Found in the first real seon_done (2026-11-15) [M result/c510721e]: two ways to read a program schema in a process: seon.contract/options (a binding, the branch layer an agent holds, malli's default, the built registry) and bare (malli.core/schema k), malli's default registry, which is main's (seon.program/hold-registry!). On an agent's branch the bare one silently answers main's schema: in agent k159570deaf49ad6, :seon.repl/answer's keys read [:handle :forms :value ...] through options and [:handle :value :ns ...] bare. The pilot's seon.db/response-order read it bare and turned seon.ctx.wire-test/what-the-model-reads-is-the-same-with-result-entities red on its branch; data's own check made the same call and filed a registry gap that did not exist (seon.program/an-imported-schema-change-is-the-agents-registry, deleted). src holds 3 bare reads, all in seon.config with an explicit registry; the class is in code agents write at their REPL. PRIOR WORK: seon.contract/layers and options, branch-registry (the agent's schema slot read at each lookup), seon.program/hold-registry! (root's and the fork server's default). FIX by construction [H]: an agent process's malli default registry is the branch layer over the built one (a composite of the dynamic registry, branch-registry and the built registry, never the default itself, which would recurse), set once at the agent's start, so a bare read on a branch reads the branch; loaders on throwaway dbs bind the dynamic registry and are unchanged. A law would only mark the call; construction removes the second way. COST: none a lookup beyond options' own (one slot read). PROOF to write, red first: in an agent whose branch changed a schema entry, (malli.core/schema k) answers the branch's form. STATE 2026-11-16 (data, handover): the fix is built on branch jolt/data, commit 8bbbc8d441 (seon.agent/init! holds (seon.program/hold-registry! (seon.contract/branch-over (seon.program/schema-registry (seon.program/schema-changes db0)))); seon.contract/branch-over; docs/source.md schemas at the REPL). It is held back: seon.agent-test/an-agents-start-costs-the-same-however-long-its-root-has-run is RED with it, begin-run! measured 6.2 / 8.1 / 9.5 MB at 10 / 100 / 1000 agents a root has run: the start cost grows with root's age. NEXT: find what begin-run! reads that grows with the agents a root ran (read it at the REPL on a root that ran 1000), fix that, then land the fix with its proofs."}
 {:seon.task/name "a-functions-contract-checks-against-its-branchs-registry"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by data, 2026-11-16 [M result/ab86d24d at a fresh agent on main]: seon.task/move-tx refused :reprove, its contract compiled at load against the binary's registry (the outcome enum with :behind, the binary predating C2's schema change, which main holds). A function's contract is compiled once against the registry the binary baked, so a schema change on main or a branch never reaches the functions already instrumented: a test or agent on that branch is refused a value its own schema allows. PRIOR WORK: seon.contract's branch-registry and layers (each lookup reads the agent's schema slot), seon.db/held-schema (a process's Datahike schema from the same slot, d90457cd2c), the instrumentation's compile at definition (docs/checks.md#contracts-armed-at-definition). PLAN: a contract names its schema by key, looked up through layers at the call, or the instrumented functions whose schemas reach a moved entry are armed again when the slot moves (one pass over those functions, never all). COST: estimate one registry lookup a call (what layers already pays) or O(functions reaching the moved entries) once a schema move. Proof, red first: a function whose contract names an enum a branch widens accepts the branch's new value at that branch's agent."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.contract-test/a-functions-contract-checks-against-its-branchs-registry}}
